-- title:
--   q-expansion criterion at a supersingular point, H₁ level
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and let $\ell_g$ be a prime with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ dividing $M'$ (and lying in the prime factors of $M'$). Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q$ a nonunit of $A$, let $k_0$ be an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ and put $A_0 := A \cap k_0$ (the comap of $A$ along $k_0 \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$); assume $\pi_0 \in k_0$ lies in $A$, that $A_0$ is a henselian discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$ and algebraically closed residue field, and that every element of $A$ is congruent, modulo the maximal ideal of $A$, to an element of $k_0 \cap A$. Assume $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $(q\ell_g)$-th root of unity admitting a ring embedding $k_0 \to \mathbb C$ sending $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/(q\ell_g)}$. Let $H_1 \le (\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ be the intersection of the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$ with the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$, and let $K \subseteq k_0(\!(q)\!)$ be the intermediate field generated over $k_0$ by the coefficientwise image of the function field $\mathtt{xHFunctionField}(q^2M', H_1)$ of level $q^2M'$ and character group $H_1$, with $A_0 \to K$ compatible with $A_0 \to k_0 \to K$. Let $j \in K$ be nonzero with Laurent expansion the image of the $q$-expansion $\mathtt{jq}$. Write $C' := \mathtt{chartAlgFin}\,A_0\,K\,j$, the $A_0$-subalgebra $\mathtt{chartAlg}\,A_0\,K\,\{j\}$ of $K$, and let $y'$ be a maximal ideal of $C'$ containing the image of $\pi_0$ such that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C' \to \Omega$ with kernel $y'$, the element $\varphi(j)$ lies in $\mathtt{ssJSet}\,q\,\Omega$, i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $\varphi(j)$ has no nonzero point killed by $q$. Then any $h \in C'$ all of whose Laurent coefficients (over all $n \in \mathbb Z$) lie in the image in $k_0$ of the maximal ideal of $A_0$ belongs to $y'$.
--
--   This is the $q$-expansion principle in the form needed at a supersingular point of the $H_1$-level modular curve: integrality of all expansion coefficients in the maximal ideal of the small constant ring forces vanishing at the chosen supersingular maximal ideal of the $j$-finite chart. It is the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-guarded (trivial-diamond) version of the corresponding full-auxiliary-level statement, and feeds the auxiliary level-one analysis on the two-chart integral model.
